window.CY100EN={"Atherinomorus lacunosus": "The hardyhead silverside is a small coastal species, quite common in our waters. It is found close to shore, mainly in shallow bays and harbours, down to 10 metres. Large schools of juveniles flood our coasts in the summer months, while larger individuals become very noticeable in September and October. It is usually about 10 centimetres long, though it can reach 18 centimetres. It feeds on zooplankton and small benthic invertebrates. It breeds in our waters during the summer months. It is caught from the shore with rods and special small nets (atherinologous), and its flesh is very tasty. Besides A. lacunosus, which is a Lessepsian species (it entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal), two endemic silverside species also live in our waters, Atherina hepsetus and Atherina boyeri. These look similar to A. lacunosus but are less common.", "Epinephelus caninus": "The dogtooth grouper, known in some parts of Cyprus as \"asprovlachos\", \"vlachosfyrida\" or \"ammatas\", is a bottom-dwelling species found on muddy and sandy bottoms at depths of 40 to 400 metres. It is one of the largest species in its family, reaching 150 centimetres in length and 70 kilos in weight, although its usual length does not exceed 80 centimetres and its weight 6 kilos. Many fishermen confuse it with the white grouper (Epinephelus aeneus), which it resembles in some respects. It feeds on fish, cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid), crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, etc.) and other invertebrates. It is a protogynous hermaphrodite: it first matures as a female and later changes into a male. It is caught with heavy longlines, deep handlines and, more rarely, with static nets and bottom trawls, and its flesh is considered of very good quality. The dogtooth grouper is quite vulnerable to fishing, mainly because it is easy to catch and because of its slow reproduction and growth. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the dogtooth grouper in the Mediterranean at 45 centimetres.", "Dasyatis centroura": "The roughtail stingray is among the species frequently found in Cyprus. The term \"vati\", as used by the island's inhabitants, does not refer only to this species but to all cartilaginous rays. D. centroura is the ray caught most often, which is why it was chosen for this book. It is quite large, and its full wingspan can exceed 2 metres. It is strictly a bottom-dweller, found on sandy and muddy habitats down to 200 metres, although it is usually in shallower water, between 10 and 50 metres. In the summer months it appears more often along the coast, probably searching for food. It feeds with the help of its large fins (moving them up and down), searching the sand for its food, which consists of small bottom-dwelling fish, invertebrates, molluscs, crustaceans and smaller rays. It has a diamond-shaped body with two large triangular pectoral fins and a rounded snout. Like most rays, it has a very long tail, longer than its body. The tail carries rows of many small spines and a long, sharp, venomous spine at its base, which in several cases can be double. From late summer until late autumn it approaches the coast, where after a four-month gestation it gives birth to its young. The number of free-living young born per breeding season ranges from 2 to 4. The species is considered harmful to humans, not because of the numerous spines along its tail but because of the venomous spine at its base. It is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, longlines and spearguns. Although it is counted among the dangerous fish, it is not discarded, since it is sold for its large fleshy fins. Its commercial value is not particularly high.", "Belone belone": "The garfish is a coastal, epipelagic species, quite common in Cyprus. It is found from the shore to the open sea, down to 10 metres. In the warm months of the year this species forms schools that flood our coasts. The garfish can reach 90 centimetres in length and exceed 1 kilo in weight, but its length usually does not exceed 50 centimetres. It is a very capable hunter and catches its prey with sudden sideways movements of its beak-like mouth. It feeds mainly on small fish. It breeds in Cypriot waters in the summer months. The garfish is caught mainly by amateur fishermen in our country with a rod from the shore or by trolling, using \"matasines\". Matasines, or threads, are bundles of thread, usually silk, that tangle in the garfish's small, sharp teeth. The garfish, mistaking the matasina for a small swimming fish, attacks it and is thus caught on the angler's line. Many amateur fishermen use the garfish as live bait when trolling, to catch larger predators such as \"mineri\", the common dentex, the leerfish and others. The flesh of the garfish is very tasty and its commercial value is quite high.", "Rhinobatos cemiculus": "The blackchin guitarfish, known to the general public in Cyprus as \"viola\", is among the species found relatively often in Cyprus. The term \"viola\" does not refer only to this species but also to Rhinobatos rhinobatos, which also occurs along the island's coasts. In most cases, even the fishermen themselves, whether from indifference or because of the similarity in colouring, biology and shape between the two species, do not tell them apart. The guitarfish is counted among the cartilaginous fish, but it has a rhomboid body shape, more elongated than that of most rays. It is strictly a bottom-dweller, found on sandy and muddy habitats down to 100 metres. It usually moves slowly near the bottom or lies hidden in the sand. Its length ranges between 40 and 80 centimetres, although it can exceed one and a half metres. It feeds with the help of its fins (moving them up and down), searching the sand for food, which consists of small bottom-dwelling fish and invertebrates. The guitarfish breeds once or twice a year by ovoviviparity and gives birth to 4 to 8 young each time. It is caught with longlines or static nets and sometimes with bottom trawls. It has negligible commercial value and in most cases is discarded.", "Phycis phycis": "The forkbeard, known as \"garos\", \"pylos\", \"malachtos\" or \"salouvardos\", is a bottom-dwelling species that lives at depths of 15 to 600 metres on rocky substrates adjoining areas of sand or mud. It is a nocturnal species and usually hides in rock cavities during the day. The forkbeard can exceed 60 centimetres in length and 3 kilos in weight, although its usual length does not exceed 40 centimetres and its weight 1 kilo. It feeds mostly on small fish and invertebrates. It breeds from January to May. The forkbeard is fished with static nets, bottom trawls, longlines and handlines. It is not considered a very good quality fish, and for this reason its market price is low.", "Solea solea": "The common sole is a benthic fish that lives in close contact with muddy or sandy substrates, down to 200 metres. It has a characteristic shape, with a strongly flattened body and no bilateral symmetry. Its length can reach and exceed 50 centimetres, although it usually does not exceed 30 centimetres. It is a nocturnal species and feeds mainly on benthic invertebrates. The sole breeds from January to April, peaking in February. In our waters it is fished with bottom trawls and, more rarely, with static nets, fine handlines and rods. Large individuals have very tasty flesh and therefore fetch a high price on the market. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the common sole S. solea in the Mediterranean at 20 centimetres. Besides S. solea, several other species in our waters share the same common name and have similar morphological and biological characteristics. The most important of these are Arnoglossus laterna, Bothus podas and Citharus linguatula. A. laterna reaches 20 centimetres in length and lives down to 1000 metres. B. podas reaches 45 centimetres in length and is found more often in shallow waters and more rarely down to 400 metres. Finally, C. linguatula reaches 25 centimetres in length and is found down to 200 metres. These species breed in the spring and summer months.", "Boops boops": "The bogue, known in some parts of Cyprus as \"goppa\" or \"boppa\", is a very common fish in the waters of our island. It is a semi-pelagic species found on a variety of substrates (muddy, rocky, sandy, vegetated, etc.) and thrives throughout the water column. It is found at depths between 1 and 350 metres, but you are most likely to meet it between 5 and 100 metres. The bogue is a very sociable fish and forms large schools of its own kind. During the night it rises towards the surface of the water. Its length usually ranges from 10 to 15 centimetres, although it can exceed 30 centimetres. It is an omnivorous fish, but feeds mainly on crustaceans and planktonic organisms. In Cyprus the species starts breeding in February and finishes in April. The bogue is a hermaphrodite and shows protogynous behaviour: it first matures as a female and later changes into a male. It matures sexually for the first time when its length reaches about 13 centimetres. It is easily caught with nets and handlines, and it is a target species for bottom trawls because of its abundance and its social nature. Since it is usually small and generally abundant in our areas, it has little commercial value. Large individuals of this species have higher commercial value and are considered tastier.", "Chlorophthalmus agassizi": "The shortnose greeneye is a very common deep-water species that lives in schools on muddy substrates, from 50 to 1000 metres deep. It reaches 40 centimetres in length, although it usually does not exceed 20 centimetres. It feeds mainly on small deep-water benthic invertebrates. The greeneye is a simultaneous hermaphrodite, meaning that eggs and sperm are present in the same individual. Its exact breeding season is not known. The greeneye is caught almost exclusively with bottom trawls. Its flesh is of average quality and its commercial value is low.", "Coris julis": "The rainbow wrasse is a benthic species that lives mainly in rocky or vegetated areas. It is found mainly in the coastal zone at depths from 1 to 60 metres. Adult males may also be seen in deeper water, mainly in the winter months. It lives alone or forms colonies of its own kind. Its length usually ranges from 15 to 25 centimetres. At night, or when it feels threatened, it hides in holes in the rocks or in the sand. It feeds on small gastropods, molluscs, sea urchins, crustaceans and benthic invertebrates in general. Juveniles in particular also form a symbiotic relationship with other marine organisms and act as cleaners, rid those organisms of annoying parasites while securing food for themselves. The species breeds from May until the end of August. There is sexual dimorphism in this species, with males having more intense colours than females. The difference between the sexes becomes more pronounced after adulthood. Males have a characteristic bold spot at the base of their dorsal fin, and their first three dorsal rays are longer. The rainbow wrasse is a hermaphrodite and shows protogynous behaviour: within usually one year it matures sexually as a female and later changes into a male. Rainbow wrasses longer than 18 centimetres are as a rule male. It is caught with static nets, handlines, rods and \"skarkes\". Some also use it as live bait for larger prey. It has very little commercial value and in most cases is discarded. Besides C. julis, there is another species in Cypriot waters with the same common name, Thalassoma pavo (the ornate wrasse). It reaches 20 centimetres in length and has biological and morphological characteristics similar to C. julis. T. pavo breeds from late June to early August. A distinguishing feature between the sexes in this species, besides colour, is the tail. Specifically, the outer rays of the males' caudal fin are longer than those of the females.", "Gobius niger": "The black goby is a small benthic species that lives on almost all types of bottom and down to 75 metres, most often down to 50 metres. It is a small fish and its length does not exceed 15 centimetres. The goby feeds on small crustaceans and molluscs found close to the substrate. It breeds in the spring months. The goby has no commercial value, but it is often caught with bottom trawls, fine longlines, handlines and rods. Some also use it as live bait for larger prey. Besides Gobius niger, other goby species live in Cypriot waters, the most common perhaps being Gobius cruentatus, which reaches 18 centimetres in length and is light reddish in colour. All gobies have a similar shape, so identifying the species is quite difficult.", "Trachinus draco": "The greater weever is a benthic species that lives quite close to the bottom and is often found along the coasts of Cyprus. It is found mainly on sandy and muddy bottoms, at depths ranging from 1 to 150 metres. The length of this fish usually ranges between 15 and 30 centimetres, although it sometimes exceeds 40 centimetres. The weever has a relatively large mouth that tilts clearly upwards, to make it easier to seize prey quickly, since it spends most of its time camouflaged in the sand with only its head exposed. In the summer months it comes into the shallows, where it hides in the sand all day and lies in wait, well camouflaged, for small bottom-dwelling fish, mainly gobies. Like most fish, it hunts at night. The weever feeds mainly on small invertebrates and fish. The breeding season in our area takes place in the spring months and can extend to the end of summer. All weever species have similar morphological and biological characteristics and are caught with the same gear. Besides Trachinus draco, other species in Cypriot waters share the common name weever. These are Echiichthys vipera, Trachinus araneus and Trachinus radiatus. Of these, T. radiatus is perhaps the most common. T. radiatus is quite large, with a length ranging from 10 to 30 centimetres, and it can exceed 40 centimetres. It occurs at depths of 30 to 150 metres and has the same feeding preferences as T. draco. E. vipera resembles T. draco in shape and telling the two apart is quite difficult. The weever is harmful to humans, since its first dorsal fin consists of five hard spines with venom glands at their base. Besides the venomous dorsal fin, weevers also have two equally venomous spines on the sides, one on each gill cover. Their stings are very painful and cause serious complications. The weever is very tasty, but because of its appearance it has little commercial value, in proportion to the size of the fish. It is caught with static nets, bottom trawls, spearguns, longlines and handlines.", "Tetrapturus belone": "The Mediterranean spearfish, known in Cyprus as \"zargana\" or \"vasilopsaro\", is a relatively large pelagic species that resembles the swordfish in shape because of the long, pointed extension of its upper jaw. A mature adult can reach 2 metres in length, although it usually ranges between 50 centimetres and 1 metre. The spearfish is a highly migratory species and prefers to be in the open sea in deep water, where it moves from the surface down to 200 metres. At times it is also found in shallower water, where it comes to look for food or to breed. Although the spearfish occurs throughout the Mediterranean, it is better known in the north-western part. In summer it migrates north, seeking a temperate climate and colder waters, while in the autumn months it migrates south, seeking warmer waters rich in food. The spearfish is an excellent predator and likes to move in pairs, which it does to increase its hunting efficiency. In search of food it can travel many kilometres and dive several metres deep. It feeds on various fish, invertebrates and molluscs, but mainly on squid, mackerel and sardines. The paradox is that, although it is one of the most effective predators in the marine ecosystem, it uses no special technique but persistently follows its prey, reaching high speed and skill and catching it inevitably. Often it kills its victims with its bill, especially if they are bottom-dwelling organisms. The spearfish's breeding season runs from early February to mid June. It is caught with surface longlines, surface trolling lines and sometimes with spearguns and static nets. It has relatively low commercial value.", "Trigloporus lastoviza": "In Cyprus there are several fish species known by the common name \"caponi\" or \"petinos\" (gurnard). They resemble each other closely, and because of their low commercial value and identical shape the species are not properly told apart. T. lastoviza is perhaps the one found most often in Cypriot waters. Besides this species, the most common gurnards in Cyprus are Trigla lucerna, Aspitrigla cuculus, Lepidotrigla cavillone and Trigla lyra. The streaked gurnard is purely a bottom-dwelling species and occurs in areas with rocky substrates, as well as in sandy-muddy areas, usually down to 150 metres. It lives close to the substrate, digging in the sand to find food. Its length usually ranges from 12 to 20 centimetres, although it can exceed 40 centimetres. It feeds exclusively on small invertebrates. When it feels threatened, the gurnard unfolds its large colourful pectoral fins to look more imposing and possibly to deter would-be predators from attacking. For most of the time it lies still on the sand, where, like all gurnards, it moves with the help of its pectoral fins, at the front of which there are three separate ray-like false legs. These false legs also help detect invertebrates hidden in the sand. The species can also form small schools in mid-water. It breeds between June and August. Gurnards are caught incidentally with bottom trawls and occasionally with static nets, longlines and handlines, and have little commercial value.", "Pseudocaranx dentex": "The white trevally, known as \"karangida\" or \"kokkali\", is a coastal species found mainly at depths of less than 30 metres, although it can also be found deeper, down to 200 metres. It forms large schools and its presence is stronger in areas with shoals or shipwrecks. Juveniles of this species are often seen swimming next to larger fish of other species. The trevally can exceed 1 metre in length, although it usually does not exceed 50 centimetres and 3 kilos in weight. It feeds mainly on planktonic microorganisms and benthic invertebrates. It breeds in the summer months. The flesh of the trevally is considered of very good quality. It is fished mainly with static nets, rods and spearguns.", "Mugil cephalus": "The flathead mullet, known as \"kefalos\" or \"poklanos\", is a benthopelagic species that usually moves in groups over sandy, muddy or mixed substrates, mainly down to 20 metres, although we sometimes find it in deeper water. It is one of the most common fish along our country's coastline, since it moves in large numbers in shallow water, and it seems to prefer closed bays and harbours with murky and often unclean water. The mullet is a euryhaline species, since it can live and breed in fresh water as well. It can exceed 1 metre in length and 7 kilos in weight, but fish of this species caught in our waters usually do not exceed 2 kilos. The mullet is more active during the day, although this is not a rule. It feeds mainly on algae and microorganisms from the substrate, as well as organic particles in the water column. It breeds from July to October. It is fished with static nets, spearguns, rods and special traps. In Cyprus many amateurs are passionate about fishing mullet with a rod from the shore. Small individuals are also used by many as live bait for catching large predators. Its flesh is quite tasty, especially when the fish is caught in clean water, and it fetches quite a high price on the market. Besides M. cephalus, other species in our waters share the common name mullet. The most common are Liza aurata and Chelon labrosus. The golden grey mullet L. aurata reaches 50 centimetres in length and 2 kilos in weight and has a characteristic yellow spot on each gill cover. The thicklip grey mullet C. labrosus reaches 60 centimetres in length and 3 kilos in weight and has a characteristically thick upper lip with horny papillae.", "Squalus acanthias": "The spurdog, known as \"kokkalaris\" or \"akanthias\", is perhaps the most successful shark species worldwide, since it occurs in most seas in relatively large numbers. It is a benthopelagic small dogfish that we meet quite often in the waters around our island. It is usually found at depths from 5 to 150 metres, although the species has been recorded at depths exceeding 1000 metres. In addition, the spurdog can survive in brackish water. The length of this fish usually ranges from 50 to 110 centimetres and its weight from 1 to 3 kilos, although it can reach 9 kilos and its length can exceed 150 centimetres. It is a social species and forms packs according to its sex and size, although larger seasonal packs made up of males and females of various sizes are also seen. Its diet, as with most sharks, is varied, but because of its small size it is mainly limited to small bony fish, crustaceans, gastropods, cephalopods and jellyfish. Although it is a shark, it is not a threat to humans, but because of its spines and flexibility it can become dangerous if caught alive in nets or on hooks. The spurdog breeds in winter. During gestation, which lasts about 22 months, the female carries the eggs inside her body. Each female can give birth to 1 to 20 pups (ovoviviparity). The newborns are self-sufficient and fully independent from the first moment of their lives, and their size ranges from 18 to 30 centimetres. At sexual maturity, males are 60 to 70 centimetres long, while females are 75 to 90 centimetres. The spurdog is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, longlines and handlines. It is not considered a discard, but it is often discarded because of its size and the two spines on its back. Nevertheless, it is tasty and very beneficial. Its commercial value is relatively low.", "Anthias anthias": "The swallowtail seaperch is a small bottom-dwelling species that prefers hard substrates at depths from 30 to 200 metres. It is often seen living in small groups at the entrance to or inside rock cavities. It is a small species whose length rarely exceeds 15 centimetres. It is a nocturnal species and feeds mostly on crustaceans and small fish. It is a protogynous hermaphrodite. In a school all the fish are female except the largest, which is male. When the male dies, the largest of the females automatically turns into a male. It breeds in spring. The seaperch has no commercial value, but it is often caught as bycatch in fishermen's static nets.", "Polyprion americanus": "The wreckfish, known as \"kolaouzos\" or \"vlachos\", is a bottom-dwelling species found mainly on muddy and sandy bottoms, usually at depths from 50 to 500 metres and sometimes down to 1000 metres. Small fish of the species, which have characteristic white spots, are often seen swimming near floating objects. It reaches 2 metres in length and 100 kilos in weight, although its usual length does not exceed 70 centimetres and its weight 8 kilos. It feeds on fish, cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid) and crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, etc.). The wreckfish breeds in the summer months. It is caught with heavy longlines and deep handlines, and its flesh is considered of very good quality. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the wreckfish in the Mediterranean at 45 centimetres.", "Scomber japonicus": "The chub mackerel is one of the most common small pelagic species that flood our coasts in the summer months, down to 300 metres. It looks very much like the Atlantic mackerel, except that it has dark lines or spots on the belly area. It also differs from the Atlantic mackerel in that it has a swim bladder, which the mackerel does not. Like the mackerel, the chub mackerel can reach 50 centimetres in length, although it rarely exceeds 30 centimetres. It is a migratory species that forms large schools of equal-sized fish. It feeds on smaller pelagic fish species as well as pelagic invertebrates. It breeds in the summer months, mainly in August. The chub mackerel is caught mainly with static nets and bottom trawls, as well as with fine trolling lines, handlines and \"tsapari\" (feather rigs). Although its flesh is of relatively good quality and very beneficial, it does not have great commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the chub mackerel in the Mediterranean at 18 centimetres.", "Cepola macrophthalma": "The red bandfish is found relatively often in Cypriot waters. It is a benthic fish and lives on sandy-muddy substrates at depths of 15 to 100 metres. It forms small schools of its own kind or lives alone in deep burrows, but sometimes it can also be found in mid-water. Its length usually ranges from 20 to 35 centimetres, although it can reach 70 centimetres. It feeds on crustaceans and small invertebrates. The breeding season in our area runs between March and November. The bandfish is caught mainly with bottom trawls and occasionally with static nets. It has no commercial value, which is why it is discarded.", "Siganus rivulatus": "The marbled spinefoot, known in Cyprus as \"aspri kourkouna\" or \"aspri prosfygoula\", is a fish that in recent years has often appeared along our country's coasts. It is a benthic species and is found mainly in areas with meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). It may also be found on rocky bottoms not far from the shore, at depths between 1 and 60 metres, but usually not deeper than 30 metres. The spinefoot's length can exceed 40 centimetres, but it rarely exceeds 25 centimetres. This fish is not native but a Lessepsian migrant, which first appeared in Cypriot waters around 1869, after the opening of the Suez Canal linking the Red Sea with the Mediterranean. At first these fish were caught sporadically and were not known to the island's general public. In recent years, with rising temperatures, the fish has become established and is abundant along the Cypriot coasts. It is characterised by very thin, sharp, venomous spines along the continuous dorsal fin. It is strictly herbivorous, feeding on seaweed and algae. The spinefoot is a migratory species and often forms large schools that can exceed 100 individuals. The breeding season starts in May and ends around the end of August. It matures sexually at about 2 years of age, when its length is around 14 centimetres. It is caught with rods, static nets, encircling nets, \"skarkes\" and spearguns. It has quite high commercial value, in proportion to the size of the fish. It is considered a delicacy, mainly by residents of the eastern areas of Cyprus.", "Siganus luridus": "The dusky spinefoot, known in Cyprus as \"mavri kourkouna\" or \"mavri prosfygoula\", is a benthic species and occurs in the same areas as the marbled spinefoot, but in smaller numbers. It too migrated to our region from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal. It is a relatively smaller fish than the marbled spinefoot. A mature adult has a length between 10 and 20 centimetres, although it can reach 30 centimetres. Like the marbled spinefoot, it is characterised by very thin, sharp, venomous spines along the continuous dorsal fin. It is strictly herbivorous, feeding on seaweed and algae. It is a migratory fish and often forms schools, which, compared with those of the marbled spinefoot, consist of fewer individuals. Mature individuals may also occur alone. It has the same dietary preferences as the marbled spinefoot. The breeding season starts in May and ends around the end of August. The dusky spinefoot matures sexually when its length is between 12 and 16 centimetres. It is caught with rods, static nets, encircling nets, \"skarkes\" and spearguns. It has quite high commercial value, in proportion to the size of the fish. It is considered a delicacy, mainly by residents of the eastern areas of Cyprus.", "Apogon imberbis": "The cardinal fish, known as \"krommydi\" or \"kremmydopsaro\", is a small bottom-dwelling fish that prefers hard substrates at depths of 10 to 200 metres. It is often seen living in small groups at the entrance to or inside rock cavities. In summer it migrates to shallow water, around 15 metres deep. Its length rarely exceeds 15 centimetres and usually ranges around 10 centimetres. It is a nocturnal species, and at night it leaves the cavities where it lives in search of food. It feeds mainly on small invertebrates, larvae and fish. The cardinal fish breeds in our waters in the summer months. After fertilisation, the male takes a number of eggs into his mouth and guards them there until they hatch. The cardinal fish has no commercial value, but it is often caught as bycatch in static nets.", "Coryphaena hippurus": "The common dolphinfish, known as \"kynigos\" or \"akkanomouttas\", is a pelagic species well known to professional and amateur fishermen in our country, particularly for its beautiful colours when alive and for the vigour with which it fights once hooked. It is quite a large species, since it can exceed 1 metre in length and 20 kilos in weight, although in our waters it usually does not exceed 10 kilos. It swims mainly in open water and at times near the coast, from the surface down to 100 metres. It usually forms schools, although the largest individuals live either alone or in pairs. Often the dolphinfish follows ships or likes to approach large floating objects. It is an excellent hunter that rushes at its prey in a flash at speeds above 40 knots. It feeds mainly on epipelagic fish, squid and crustaceans. The dolphinfish breeds in our waters in the spring months. It is fished with surface longlines, surface trolling lines and lures. It is considered a good-quality fish and its market value is relatively high.", "Lagocephalus sceleratus": "The silverstripe blaasop, known as \"lagokefalos\", \"kouneli\" or \"kounelopsaro\", is a Lessepsian species that has been appearing strongly in our country's waters in recent years. It is known to our fishermen and to the general public, mainly because of the damage it causes to fishermen's gear and catches, and because of the danger of eating it, owing to the toxin it contains. The blaasop moves in schools over all types of substrate, from the bottom to the surface, and reaches depths of 100 metres. It is an opportunistic predator and feeds on a wide variety of organisms, such as cephalopods, crustaceans and fish. Very often it attacks fish that are already caught in nets or on hooks, causing damage with its powerful beak. It does not hesitate to attack even individuals of its own species. It breeds in the summer months, from June to August. Unfortunately, the blaasop is now one of the most frequent catches of our country's fishermen. It is caught with static nets, longlines, handlines, trolling lines and lures. Its flesh, although tasty according to some fishermen who have tried it, contains tetrodotoxin and can be a source of food poisoning, with highly associated mortality rates. In any case, the public should avoid eating this species, even if some claim to have tried it without consequences. Besides, the toxicity of this species has been shown to vary according to season, age, breeding stage, area, etc. The marketing of this fish is expressly prohibited by European regulations. Besides L. sceleratus, there are at least three other species of the same family in our country. These are L. suezensis, which resembles L. sceleratus but is considerably smaller, L. spadiceus, and Sphoeroides pachygaster, which lives in deeper water. Although these species have not been shown to be toxic, it is advisable for the public to avoid eating them.", "Dicentrarchus labrax": "The European seabass is a coastal species that lives on all types of bottom down to 50 metres. It often enters closed and shallow bays or even the murky waters of harbours to hunt for food. Young fish form schools, while larger ones usually live alone or in very small groups. The seabass can reach 1 metre in length and 12 kilos in weight, but fish caught in our waters usually do not exceed 2 kilos. It is an excellent hunter and feeds on crustaceans and molluscs when young and more on fish as an adult. This species shows a kind of territorial behaviour, since the same individuals appear to hunt in the same defined areas for long periods. The seabass breeding season is January, February and March. The seabass is fished with static nets, rods, trolling lines and spearguns, and more rarely with fine longlines. Most of the fish caught in our seas are those that occasionally escape from fish farms. Wild seabass are quite rare and differ from farmed ones in having a bigger and thicker head in proportion to the rest of the body. In any case, the seabass is considered a fish of excellent quality. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the seabass in the Mediterranean at 25 centimetres.", "Lichia amia": "The leerfish is mostly a coastal species, found mainly in surface waters down to 50 metres. It is quite a large species, since it can reach 2 metres in length and 50 kilos in weight, although it usually does not exceed 1 metre in length and 10 kilos in weight. The leerfish is an excellent hunter and does not hesitate to enter very shallow water or even harbours to catch its prey. It feeds mostly on fish, which it often hunts spectacularly at the surface of the water. The leerfish breeds in the summer months. It is caught mainly by amateur fishermen in our country with a rod from the shore, by trolling from a boat and with spearguns, while few fish of this species are caught in professional fishermen's static nets. Its flesh is of quite good quality and its commercial value quite high.", "Trachinotus ovatus": "The derbio, known as \"litsoui\", is a coastal species found down to 200 metres. It usually moves in small groups very close to the shore, over sandy bottoms with clear water. It can reach 70 centimetres in length and 3 kilos in weight, but its length usually does not exceed 35 centimetres. It feeds on benthic organisms such as molluscs, crustaceans (shrimps and crabs) and small fish. It breeds in the summer months. It is caught mainly with static nets, rods, light trolling lines and spearguns, and its flesh is considered quite good.", "Pagellus erythrinus": "The common pandora, known as \"lythrini\", remains one of the species in adequate supply on our island, although fishing production in recent years suggests that the population of the species has declined significantly. It is a benthic species and lives on rocky and sandy-muddy substrates at depths of 20 to 200 metres. Adults usually prefer depths greater than 20 metres, while juveniles prefer less than 50 metres. In the winter months the pandora migrates to deeper water, while the opposite happens in the summer months. It is a solitary fish or forms small schools of its own kind, especially in autumn and spring, when it also breeds. The pandora's length usually ranges from 10 to 25 centimetres. In rare cases it can reach 2 kilos and its length can exceed 40 centimetres. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds on invertebrates, mainly crustaceans and worms, as well as small fish. As mentioned above, the species breeds in two seasons, autumn and spring. The pandora is a protogynous hermaphrodite, with a length at first maturity of about 13 centimetres. It changes into a male, usually in its third year of life, when its length reaches about 16 centimetres. It is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, longlines and handlines and has high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the pandora in the Mediterranean at 15 centimetres.", "Uranoscopus scaber": "The stargazer, known as \"lychnos\" or \"myxis\", is a very common species in the coastal waters of Cyprus. It is a benthic fish and lives on sandy-muddy substrates at depths of 15 to 250 metres. The length of the species usually ranges from 10 to 20 centimetres, although it can exceed 30 centimetres. The stargazer is a fairly effective bottom predator. It is usually buried in the sand with only its eyes and its upward-slanting mouth exposed, which makes it possible to seize its prey. It has a large, conspicuous venomous spine behind its two gill covers. Between its lower jaw and its tongue it has a fleshy red protrusion that serves as bait for unsuspecting prey. Studies have shown that the stargazer can produce electric discharges of up to 50 volts through an organ in a sac just behind the eyes. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds on invertebrates and mainly on small fish, which it swallows whole. The species breeds in summer. It is caught with static nets, bottom trawls and handlines. Its commercial value is quite low.", "Scomberomorus commerson": "The narrow-barred Spanish mackerel, known as \"makerel\" or \"ravdoto skoumbri\", is a pelagic species that lives mainly in coastal waters and at depths of no more than 200 metres. In some areas of our country it is also called \"vasilopsaro\", like the spearfish (Tetrapturus belone), although the two species are not related. It is a Lessepsian species, that is, an Indo-Pacific species that migrated into the Mediterranean through the Suez Canal. In recent years it has been caught more and more often in our waters. It is quite a large fish, since it can reach 2 metres in length and 50 kilos in weight. However, individuals of this species caught in our waters usually do not exceed 10 kilos. The Spanish mackerel hunts its prey, usually moving in small groups, and feeds on small fish, squid and shrimp. The breeding season of the species in our area has not yet been determined. It is caught with static nets, trolling lines and spearguns and is a very good-quality fish with quite high commercial value.", "Spicara smaris": "The picarel, known as \"marida\", is the most common fish in the sea around Cyprus, since in recent years it has made up almost 70% of the total fishing production in Cypriot territorial waters. It is a semi-pelagic species and occurs mainly in areas with rich vegetation, as well as on sandy-muddy substrates. It is a very active fish during the day and moves through the whole water column. In the evening hours it approaches the bottom. It is found at depths between 5 and 280 metres, but you are most likely to meet it between 20 and 150 metres. The picarel is a very sociable fish and forms large schools of its own kind, as well as with the related species \"tseroula\". The length of the picarel usually ranges from 7 to 15 centimetres, although during the breeding season males reach about 20 centimetres. It feeds on planktonic organisms and small invertebrates. In Cyprus the species breeds from mid-February to around the end of April. The picarel is a hermaphrodite and shows protogynous behaviour: it first matures as a female and later, usually at 2 years of age, changes into a male. The male fish, known in some areas as \"gamias\" or \"leventis\", differs in shape from the female, since it becomes larger and acquires vivid colours (see photos). During the breeding season the fish migrate to shallower water, where the female lays her eggs on the bottom, usually in seaweed, while the male fertilises them and guards them until they hatch. During this period the species' tendency to form schools is interrupted. The need to form schools returns soon afterwards, with schools of females in water around 100 metres deep and schools of males in much shallower water. After spawning, the vast majority of male picarels die. Because of its abundance and social nature, the picarel is the most important target species for Cypriot bottom trawls, and during the breeding season the males (larger and schooling in the shallows) are a target for the \"misinetika\" nets of coastal fishing. Because it is small and generally abundant in our areas, it has little commercial value.", "Centracanthus cirrus": "The curled picarel, known as \"marida amerikana\" or simply \"amerikana\", is a small benthopelagic species that belongs to the picarel family and resembles it in both shape and biology. It usually forms schools over hard substrates or gravel areas at depths down to 400 metres. It can exceed 25 centimetres in length, although most fish caught in our waters are smaller than 20 centimetres. Although there is not enough data on the diet of this species, it most likely feeds on planktonic microorganisms. The curled picarel breeds in our waters in the summer months, when these fish come closer to the coast. It is fished with bottom trawls and static nets. Its flesh is quite tasty.", "Oblada melanura": "The saddled seabream, known as \"melana\" or \"melanouri\", is among the most common bottom-dwelling fish and occurs mainly in areas with rocky bottoms or rich vegetation and clear water. It is usually found at depths between 1 and 30 metres. The saddled seabream forms large schools of its own kind of similar size, as well as with \"sorkoi\", \"charatzides\" and \"skathaires\". Schools made up of juveniles often rise to the surface foam and mid-water or find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). The length of this fish usually ranges from 15 to 20 centimetres, but it can reach 30 centimetres and its weight can exceed 1 kilo. It is an omnivorous fish and feeds mainly on small invertebrates and plants. The breeding season of the species starts in April and ends in December. The saddled seabream is a gonochoristic species, although protogynous hermaphroditism has also been observed in particular cases. It is caught with static nets, longlines, float-line rigs, \"skarkes\", light trolling lines, handlines, rods and spearguns. It has high commercial value.", "Spicara maena": "The blotched picarel, known as \"menoula\", is a fairly common fish in the waters of Cyprus. It is a semi-pelagic species and occurs in areas with rich vegetation, mainly over meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica) or on sandy-muddy substrates. It is a very active fish, especially during the day, and moves through the whole water column. In the evening hours it approaches the bottom. It is found at depths between 5 and 280 metres, but you are most likely to meet it between 5 and 150 metres. Like the other species of its family, it is a very sociable fish and forms large schools of its own kind. The length of this fish usually ranges from 12 to 20 centimetres. Males are slightly larger than females and have more intense colours. The blotched picarel feeds on planktonic organisms and small invertebrates. In Cyprus the breeding season of the species runs from April until the end of May. It is a hermaphrodite and shows protogynous behaviour: it first matures as a female and later, usually at 2 years of age, changes into a male. This species also shows sexual dimorphism, that is, the two sexes differ in shape. During the breeding season the fish migrate to shallower water, where they form even larger schools. Because of its particularly strong social nature during the breeding season, it is a target for the static nets of coastal fishing. It is also caught by bottom trawls. Because it is small and abundant along the coast for some months, it has little commercial value.", "Seriola dumerili": "The greater amberjack, known as \"mineri\" or \"magiatiko\", is a species that behaves both as a demersal and as a pelagic fish, and we find it mainly in the coastal zone. Its presence is strongest near banks, shoals, drop-offs and shipwrecks, usually at depths from 20 to 80 metres, although it is not rare at depths down to 300 metres. It is one of the largest species in its family, reaching 190 centimetres in length and 80 kilos in weight. However, individuals of the species that we meet in our waters usually do not exceed 40 kilos. The amberjack is an excellent hunter that feeds mainly on fish and cephalopods, mostly cuttlefish and squid. When young it forms large schools, which shrink in size as the fish grows. Large individuals usually occur alone or in small groups of two or three, although large schools of adults are not a rare phenomenon, particularly during the breeding season. The amberjack is a fish highly valued by fishermen, both professional and amateur, because of its size, strength, beauty and excellent taste, which give it high commercial value. Small individuals are fished with static nets, encircling nets and light surface trolling lines, while large individuals are fished with heavy nets (\"minerodichta\"), heavy longlines, bottom trolling lines, lures and spearguns. Unfortunately, the habit of this species of forming large schools at specific points on the bottom, such as shoals and shipwrecks, makes it very vulnerable to illegal dynamite fishing. Tons of amberjack were caught with dynamite in the past, when this practice was not frowned upon. Even today, however, some unscrupulous people continue to apply it illegally and wipe out huge numbers of this species, with profit as their only aim, indifferent to the future of this beautiful fish.", "Conger conger": "The conger eel, known as \"mouggri\" or \"mouggrassielo\", is a common fish in the waters of our island. It is a benthic species and lives in areas with rocky bottoms full of holes, as well as in sandy areas. It can be found at depths from 0 to 500 metres. Juveniles are found more often in coastal shallow water, while they move to deeper water when they mature. The conger is a nocturnal hunter that lies in wait for its prey. Its diet consists of fish, molluscs and crustaceans. Its length usually ranges from 50 to 150 centimetres, although it can reach 3 metres. The species breeds in the summer months. It matures sexually at an age between 5 and 15 years. Specifically, males mature when their length reaches about 75 centimetres, and females when their length reaches about 2 metres. It is caught with static nets, longlines and handlines. Because of its appearance and the special preparation required for cooking (\"galema\"), this species has very little commercial value.", "Torpedo marmorata": "The marbled torpedo ray, known as \"moudiastra\", is a bottom-dwelling species found relatively often in the waters of Cyprus. It lives on sandy or muddy bottoms, even near seaweed, at depths from 2 to 150 metres. Its length usually ranges between 20 and 60 centimetres, although it can reach 1 metre. Like almost all rays, the torpedo ray moves slowly on the bottom, searching for its food in the sand. It feeds on small benthic fish and crustaceans. The danger of the torpedo ray lies in its ability to produce electric discharges of around 200 volts, which it uses to stun or kill its prey or even to protect itself when it feels threatened. It owes its name (moudiastra, from \"numb\") to this very characteristic. It never attacks humans; on the contrary, if one approaches it, it moves away. Fishermen are at risk if it is caught alive, because it can produce electric discharges even out of the water. The breeding season of the species starts around the end of November. Females give birth once every two years, while mature males can fertilise every year. The torpedo ray is ovoviviparous. Gestation lasts 10 to 12 months and yields 5 to 32 independent young, which have the same electric properties as their parents from the first moment of their lives. It is caught by bottom trawls and the static nets of coastal fishing, but it can also be caught with longlines on sandy bottoms, particularly in winter, when it approaches shallower water. Although it is edible, it has no commercial value.", "Lithognathus mormyrus": "The striped bream, known as \"mourmouros\" or \"mourmoura\", is among the common bottom-dwelling fish of Cyprus and occurs in areas with rich vegetation or in areas with a muddy or sandy bottom. It is usually found at depths between 10 and 20 metres, but we may also meet it in shallower water or at depths down to 150 metres. It likes to form large schools of its own kind of similar size. The length of individuals usually ranges from 15 to 30 centimetres, with a maximum of 50 centimetres, and its weight can exceed 1 kilo. Its diet includes worms, molluscs and small crustaceans, which it traps by stirring the sand and digging the bottom with its snout. Because it looks for its food in the sand, where many organisms hide, it is often followed by other fish species, such as the rainbow wrasse (Coris julis), wrasses (Labridae) and the annular seabream (Diplodus annularis), which try to take advantage of the digging. The breeding season of the species starts in May and ends in September. The striped bream is a hermaphrodite and matures sexually when its length reaches 13 centimetres, initially as a male. Then, as it grows, it changes sex and becomes a female (protandry). It is caught mainly with longlines, static nets, encircling nets, handlines, spearguns and rods from the shore. It has quite high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the striped bream in the Mediterranean at 20 centimetres.", "Merluccius merluccius": "The European hake, known as \"bakaliaros\", is a bottom-dwelling species that we find mainly on muddy substrates at depths from 50 to 1000 metres. It lives close to the bottom during the day and rises higher at night to feed. The hake can exceed 1 metre in length and 10 kilos in weight, but fish caught in our waters usually do not exceed 2 kilos. It feeds mainly on fish and squid, which it hunts by rising into mid-water. In our area the hake breeds all year round, but more intensely in the winter and spring months. It is caught with longlines, bottom trawls and, less often, static nets. It has excellent-quality flesh and high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the hake in the Mediterranean at 20 centimetres. Note that our \"bakaliaros\" is not the same as the Atlantic cod (Gadus morhua) that we find frozen in supermarkets. In fact, the two species differ greatly and belong to different families.", "Pagellus bogaraveo": "The blackspot seabream, known as \"balas\", \"pallas\" or \"kefalas\", is a benthic species that lives on rocky and sandy-muddy substrates, mainly at depths of 100 to 300 metres, although it often occurs down to 600 metres or even in shallow water down to 2 metres. In general, adults prefer the deeper water, while juveniles prefer the coastal zone. It is a social fish and likes to form schools of its own kind, especially during the breeding season, when the fish migrate to shallower water to lay their eggs. Its length usually ranges from 15 to 35 centimetres. There are records in the Mediterranean of fish of this species reaching 4 kilos and a length exceeding 60 centimetres. For the most part it is a carnivorous fish and feeds on pelagic invertebrates, molluscs, fish eggs, larvae, crustaceans and small fish. The species breeds between January and May. The blackspot seabream is a protandrous hermaphrodite, with a length at first maturity between 22 and 25 centimetres at 4 or 5 years of age. It is caught with deep handlines, longlines and static nets and has high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the blackspot seabream in the Mediterranean at 33 centimetres.", "Mullus surmuletus": "The striped red mullet, known as \"barbouni\", is a small bottom-dwelling species found on stony, sandy or muddy bottoms, usually down to 100 metres. It is usually 20 to 25 centimetres long, and can reach 40 centimetres. The mullet looks for its food on the substrate with the help of its barbels, which carry taste sensors. It feeds on benthic microorganisms such as shrimps, amphipods, polychaetes, molluscs and small fish. It breeds from May to July. It is caught with bottom trawls and static nets and is a species of excellent quality and high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the red mullet in the Mediterranean at 11 centimetres.", "Brama brama": "The Atlantic pomfret, known as \"brama\" or \"lestia\", is a purely pelagic open-sea species that moves in small groups at depths down to 1000 metres. It reaches 70 centimetres in length and 4 kilos in weight. It is an opportunistic predator and feeds mainly on small fish and squid. The pomfret breeds in August and September. It is caught with surface longlines by professional fishermen, and occasionally with deep handlines by some amateurs. Its flesh is considered of quite good quality.", "Umbrina cirrosa": "The shi drum, known as \"mylokopi\", is a bottom-dwelling species that lives on sandy or mixed bottoms down to 100 metres. It can exceed 1 metre in length and 10 kilos in weight, although fish of this species caught in our waters usually do not exceed 4 kilos. The drum feeds mainly on benthic invertebrates, which it finds by digging in the soft substrate. The breeding season of the shi drum in our waters lasts from April to June. It is fished with static nets, bottom trawls, longlines, rods from the shore and spearguns. Fishermen in our country usually catch large quantities of this species after periods of heavy rainfall, when drums approach the coast to feed on the material washed down by torrents. In Cyprus there are quite a few amateur fishermen who specialise in fishing for large individuals of the species from the shore with a rod in the evening hours. Its flesh is of very good quality and its commercial value is quite high.", "Xiphias gladius": "The swordfish is a large pelagic open-sea species that we sometimes find quite close to the coast. It is a migratory and solitary species, although sometimes, especially when young, it moves in pairs or small groups. A characteristic feature of the swordfish is its very long, flattened upper jaw, which resembles a sword. It lives at depths from 0 to 800 metres, but usually down to 500 metres. The swordfish can exceed 4 metres in length and 500 kilos in weight. However, swordfish caught in our waters rarely reach 100 kilos. It is an opportunistic predator that searches for food from the surface to the bottom. It feeds on fish and squid, which it first kills with its sword. The sword is also used for defence. The swordfish has a reputation as an aggressive species and there are several reports of swordfish attacking boats or fishermen. The swordfish breeds in our waters from June to September, peaking from late June to August. It is caught with special surface longlines (swordfish longlines) and heavy handlines by some amateurs. It has excellent flesh and high commercial value. As a basic management measure for swordfish populations, a closed fishing season is set by the International Commission for the Conservation of Atlantic Tunas (ICCAT).", "Macroramphosus scolopax": "The longspine spinefish, known as \"xifioudi\", is a semi-pelagic fish that is abundant in the waters of Cyprus. It usually occurs over sandy substrates or over areas with rich vegetation. It is a small fish and its length ranges between 8 and 14 centimetres, although it can reach 20 centimetres. It is found at depths from 10 to 600 metres, but usually prefers shallower water, no deeper than 150 metres. Being a semi-pelagic species, it is found in the upper water layers. Adults are active closer to the bottom than juveniles. This is due to their different dietary preferences. Juveniles feed mainly on copepods and other pelagic invertebrates, while adults (longer than 10 centimetres) prefer benthic invertebrates. The spinefish is a very social fish and forms large schools or moves in pairs, characteristically swimming with its head tilted slightly downwards. The first of the two dorsal fins carries a serrated, venomous spine, large in relation to the total body length. Specifically, this spine is the second of the six to eight spines that make up the dorsal fin. The spinefish breeds once a year in the winter months. Although it is among the common fish of Cypriot seas, it is unknown to the general public on our island. This is because it is small and is not classed among commercial fish, but is discarded straight back into the sea. It is also because the spinefish likes to live at great depth and rarely comes into sight of bathers. It is caught with bottom trawls and sporadically with static nets. It has no commercial value.", "Dactylopterus volitans": "The flying gurnard, known as \"ornitha\", is a benthic species that lives mainly in areas with sandy-muddy substrates. It occurs mainly in the coastal zone at depths down to 80 metres. It lives alone, digging in the sand to find its food. Its length usually ranges from 20 to 30 centimetres, although it can reach half a metre. It feeds on small crustaceans, benthic invertebrates and small fish. When it feels threatened, the flying gurnard unfolds its large pectoral fins to look more imposing and possibly to scare off would-be predators. For most of the time it lies still on the sand, where it moves with the help of its pelvic fins. Its showy fins also help it glide when it is not resting on the sand. There is no information on the breeding of the species. It is caught incidentally with static nets and has little commercial value.", "Epinephelus marginatus": "The dusky grouper, known as \"orfos\" or \"rofos\", is a bottom-dwelling species found in rocky areas at depths from 5 to 200 metres. It is quite a large species, since its length can exceed 1 metre and its weight 30 kilos. Today, with the fishing pressure this species faces, large individuals are rare and the usual weight of fish caught is around 4 kilos. It is quite long-lived, since it can live for more than 50 years. The grouper shows territorial behaviour, carefully choosing its den within rock formations, which it defends and rarely leaves. Often more than one fish may share the same den. It hunts its prey mainly by ambushing close to its den. It feeds mainly on fish, cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid) and crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, etc.). The grouper is a protogynous hermaphrodite. It matures sexually at about 5 years of age as a female and at about the 15th year of its life (length about 90 cm) changes into a male. It breeds in summer. The grouper has excellent-quality flesh and high commercial value. It is caught mainly with heavy longlines, handlines, bottom trolling lines, lures and, more rarely, static nets. Small individuals are also caught quite often with \"skarkes\". The grouper is perhaps the most important catch of spearfishers, and catching a large fish of this species is considered by many to be the supreme trophy. Unfortunately, however, illegal fishing with scuba gear and spearguns, combined with the species' slow recovery rates, has led Mediterranean populations to decline. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the grouper in the Mediterranean at 45 centimetres. Some countries, such as France, have completely banned grouper fishing in certain areas, with the aim of recovering the stocks.", "Diplodus puntazzo": "The sharpsnout seabream, known in some parts of Cyprus as \"ougena\" or \"mytaki\", is not among the very common bottom-dwelling fish. It occurs mainly in areas with sandy or rocky substrates, where it likes to hide in dark holes. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). We more rarely find it deeper than 60 metres, although it can reach 150 metres. It likes to form small packs of its own kind. Its length usually ranges from 15 to 30 centimetres, although it can reach 60 centimetres and its weight can exceed one and a half kilos. It feeds mainly on seaweed, but also on worms, small molluscs and crustaceans. It is quite an active and mobile fish. The breeding season of the species starts in December and ends in January. The sharpsnout seabream is a hermaphrodite and matures sexually at 2 to 3 years of age as a male, and then changes completely into a female (protandry). It is caught mainly with fine longlines, static nets, handlines, \"skarkes\", spearguns and rods. It has quite high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the sharpsnout seabream in the Mediterranean at 18 centimetres.", "Sarda sarda": "The Atlantic bonito, known as \"palamida\", is an epipelagic, migratory species that moves in schools from the surface down to 50 metres. It reaches 90 centimetres in length and 8 kilos in weight, but usually does not exceed 50 centimetres in length and 2 kilos in weight. The bonito feeds mainly on fish, squid and shrimp, and also shows cannibalistic behaviour. It breeds in our waters from May to July. It is fished with static nets and occasionally with trolling lines. It is not rare for professional fishermen to catch large quantities of this species in a single set. The bonito has quite good flesh, but its market price is low, perhaps because production is not steady all year round and it is generally an unknown species to the general public. Besides S. sarda, there are other species in our waters with the common name palamida, the most common of which are Katsuwonus pelamis, Auxis rochei and Euthynnus alletteratus. The skipjack tuna K. pelamis is a pelagic open-sea species that is often seen hunting in large schools, churning the surface of the water. It can reach 90 centimetres in length and 25 kilos in weight, although it usually does not exceed 70 centimetres. It feeds on fish, crustaceans and cephalopods and also shows cannibalistic behaviour. It breeds from April to September and is fished with surface longlines and surface trolling lines. Its flesh is of good quality. The bullet tuna A. rochei is an epipelagic species that forms large schools of equal-sized fish, usually in coastal waters but also offshore, from the surface to 100 metres deep. It reaches 50 centimetres in length, but usually does not exceed 35 centimetres. It feeds on fish, crustaceans and squid. It has a prolonged breeding season, lasting from November to August. It is caught occasionally with static nets and its flesh is of good quality.", "Chromis chromis": "The damselfish, known as \"papathkia\" or \"kalogria\", is one of the most common fish of our country, since it floods all rocky and mixed bottoms down to 35 metres. It forms large schools that usually move in mid-water or near the bottom. It is a small fish whose length rarely exceeds 10 centimetres. It feeds mainly on planktonic and benthic microorganisms. The damselfish breeds in the summer months. A characteristic of this species is the habit of the males of guarding the eggs after fertilisation. Although it has no commercial value because of its small size, the damselfish is often caught as bycatch in static nets and \"skarkes\". Many longline enthusiasts fish for damselfish either with \"skarkes\" or with special homemade nets, to use as live bait on their longlines. In this way they manage to catch choice prey such as common dentex, groupers, red porgy and others.", "Zeus faber": "The John dory, known as \"papapetros\", \"petros\" or \"christopsaro\", is a bottom-dwelling species that lives at depths from 10 to 400 metres, usually on sandy or muddy substrates, although juveniles are often seen on rocky bottoms too. It can reach 70 centimetres in length, although it usually does not exceed 40 centimetres. The weight of this fish is disproportionately small in relation to its length, since its body is strongly flattened from side to side. This seems to help it surprise its prey, since the fish seen head-on does not betray its true dimensions. The John dory, although not a very capable swimmer, hunts its prey by approaching it slowly and, with a sudden extension of its mouth outwards, sucks in a quantity of water and its prey with it. It feeds mainly on fish, cephalopods and crustaceans. The John dory breeds in the spring months. It is fished with bottom trawls, static nets and deep handlines. Despite its, to many, off-putting appearance on the fishmonger's counter, the John dory has excellent-quality flesh and high commercial value. It is even said that the John dory is tastier two to three days after it is caught.", "Myliobatis aquila": "The common eagle ray, known to the general public in Cyprus as \"pezounopsaro\", occurs relatively often in our waters. The species Pteromylaeus bovinus also belongs to the same family and, because of its similar shape, is also known as \"pezounopsaro\". M. aquila is a large fish whose full wingspan reaches 80 centimetres, but usually does not exceed 50 centimetres. It is a bottom-dwelling species and occurs on sandy and rocky habitats or near areas with meadows of Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). The depth range in which the species is found is from 1 to 300 metres, although it is usually found in shallower water (20 to 120 metres). The eagle ray has a characteristic whip-like tail, thick at the base and gradually thinning, with a length twice the total body length of the fish. Right behind the caudal fin, the eagle ray carries a venomous sting, which is why this species is considered harmful to humans. It feeds on crustaceans, cephalopods, molluscs and worms, unearthing them from the sand with the help of its swollen snout. Bottom-dwelling fish such as the striped red mullet (Mullus surmuletus) and the red mullet (Mullus barbatus) follow eagle rays and try to take advantage of the invertebrates that the digging of this species brings to light. The eagle ray is ovoviviparous and gestation lasts about 6 to 8 months, yielding 3 to 7 young. Fertilisation takes place with the male approaching the female and holding her by biting her pectoral fin. He then slides carefully beneath the female, facing the same direction as her but upside down. Finally, he inserts his reproductive organ (clasper) into the female. This process lasts 30 to 90 seconds and takes place either on the bottom or in mid-water as the fish swim. Immediately after fertilisation, the male leaves the female, who may be fertilised in succession by other males that may be in the area.", "Serranus scriba": "The painted comber, known as \"perka\", is a very common small bottom-dwelling species that occurs on all types of bottom down to 150 metres. It can exceed 30 centimetres in length, although its length usually ranges from 10 to 20 centimetres. It feeds on small fish, crustaceans and molluscs. The painted comber is a simultaneous hermaphrodite, meaning that eggs and sperm are present in the same individual. Breeding takes place from April to August. It is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, \"skarkes\" and fine handlines, and its flesh is of average quality.", "Lophius budegassa": "The black-bellied angler, known as \"peskandritsa\" or \"peskandroula\", is a benthic species that lives on muddy or mixed substrates down to 1000 metres. It can reach 1 metre in length, but usually does not exceed 50 centimetres in length and 1 kilo in weight. The angler hunts its prey by lying in ambush, camouflaged on the substrate. It feeds mainly on fish. The breeding season of the angler has not yet been determined in our area, but it most likely takes place in the warmest months of the year. The angler is an almost exclusive catch of the bottom trawl, although it is sometimes also caught with static nets. Its flesh is of very good quality and has a high price on the market. Gourmets are certainly familiar with the tasty fish soup made from its tail.", "Mycteroperca rubra": "The mottled grouper, known in some areas of our country as \"pigga\" or \"kynigos\", is a bottom-dwelling species that prefers rocky or mixed bottoms with sharp changes in depth. We usually meet it at depths from 15 to 100 metres. It can reach 80 centimetres in length, although it usually does not exceed 50 centimetres and 3 kilos in weight. The mottled grouper, unlike most of its related species, likes to move not only near the bottom but also in mid-water, where it actively hunts its food. This habit is also reflected in its much slimmer and more streamlined body compared with its related species. It feeds mainly on fish and cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid). It is a protogynous hermaphrodite, that is, it matures sexually as a female and later changes into a male. During the breeding season it often forms large schools. The mottled grouper has flesh of average quality and is caught mainly with heavy longlines, handlines, bottom trolling lines, lures, spearguns and, with more difficulty, static nets.", "Fistularia commersonii": "The bluespotted cornetfish, known as \"pithkiavli\", \"trompeta\" or \"avlos\", is a Lessepsian species, that is, an Indo-Pacific species that migrated into the Mediterranean through the Suez Canal. It moves in large, dense schools from the shore to a depth of at least 100 metres. It can reach one and a half metres in length, although its length usually does not exceed 1 metre. It feeds on small fish and crustaceans, which it sucks in with its mouth, which works like a pump. Unfortunately, there is no data available on the breeding season of this species in our waters. The cornetfish is caught with static nets. It is considered a fish of quite good quality, although it does not have particularly high commercial value.", "Naucrates ductor": "The pilotfish is a pelagic species, known for its habit of following large pelagic species such as sharks, rays and turtles, and even boats. Juveniles often form large groups under floating seaweed, jellyfish or other objects. It may reach 70 centimetres in length, but usually does not exceed 35 centimetres. The pilotfish feeds mainly on scraps of food and parasites of the species it follows, as well as on small fish, invertebrates and molluscs. It breeds in the summer months. The pilotfish is caught mainly with static nets and its flesh is considered quite good.", "Xyrichthys novacula": "The pearly razorfish, known as \"pontikos\", is a fish that often occurs along the coasts of Cyprus. It is a benthic species and we find it in areas with rich vegetation, but mainly in areas with sandy and muddy substrates, not very far from the shore, at depths between 1 and 50 metres. The length of this fish can reach 30 centimetres, but usually ranges between 10 and 20 centimetres. It is a species that lives quite close to the bottom, and when threatened it buries itself in a flash in tiny holes it makes in the soft substrates where it lives. It feeds on small invertebrates, molluscs, crustaceans, small fish and shrimps. The breeding season of the species begins in July and ends around the end of August. The razorfish is a protogynous hermaphrodite and matures sexually at about 2 years of age, when its length is between 12 and 15 centimetres. As seen in the pictures, this species shows sexual dimorphism, with males and females differing in shape. The male always has more intense colours and is usually blue-green, while the female is usually a faded pink. It is caught mainly with static nets, fine longlines, handlines and rods. It has little commercial value, although it is quite a tasty fish.", "Raja clavata": "The thornback ray, known to the Cypriot public as \"raza\" or \"vati\", is among the species found quite often in Cyprus. The term \"raza\", as used by the island's inhabitants, does not refer only to this species but also to other cartilaginous rays of the family Rajidae. In most cases, even the fishermen themselves, whether from indifference or because of the similarity in colouring and shape of the species, do not tell them apart. So for most people it is simply another \"vati\". It is precisely for this reason, because these species do not differ much in shape, that they have been given the same general name raza, or the even more general vati. Raja clavata, known in Greece as \"kalkanovatos\", is the most common in the waters of our seas and for this reason is presented in the pages of this book. It is quite a large ray and its full wingspan reaches 110 centimetres. It is strictly a bottom-dweller and occurs in sandy and rocky habitats at depths ranging from 0 to 300 metres, although it is usually in shallower water, between 20 and 140 metres. It feeds with the help of its large fins (moving them up and down), searching the sand for its food, which consists of small bottom-dwelling fish and invertebrates. It also has a great liking for shrimps and crustaceans in general. It lays eggs and spawns all year round, with no specific breeding season (but mainly lays its eggs in winter and spring). A mature female lays about 150 eggs a year, which hatch in about 5 months. Fertilisation is achieved by a characteristic embrace of the fish, made with the help of their pectoral fins. Rays are generally considered harmful to humans because of the hooked spines they carry along their tail and body. They are often caught with bottom trawls, static nets, longlines and spearguns. Usually the thornback ray is not among the discards, because of its tasty fleshy pectoral fins, but its commercial value is low.", "Sargocentron rubrum": "The red soldierfish, known as \"rossos\", is a fish that occurs quite often along the coasts of Cyprus. It is a benthic species and we find it on rocky bottoms, not very far from the shore, at depths between 2 and 60 metres, but more often between 10 and 30 metres. The length of this fish can exceed 30 centimetres, but in Cypriot waters it usually ranges between 10 and 20 centimetres. The soldierfish is not an endemic species but a Lessepsian migrant, which entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal. It is a social fish that is active at night. During the day it usually hides in holes with individuals of its own species, but also with individuals of the genus Apogon. It feeds on polychaetes, small fish, molluscs, arthropods and, mainly, decapods. The breeding season of the species begins in June and ends around the end of August. It is caught with static nets, longlines, \"skarkes\" and rods and has very little commercial value.", "Blennius ocellaris": "The butterfly blenny, known as \"saliaros\", is a common benthic species of our seas, living mainly on hard substrates at depths down to 400 metres. It is a small fish and its length does not exceed 20 centimetres. The blenny is a nocturnal species and feeds on small invertebrates such as crustaceans and molluscs. In our waters it breeds in spring. The fertilised eggs are guarded by the male. The blenny has no commercial value, but it is often caught with rods and fine handlines or as bycatch of the bottom trawl.", "Sardina pilchardus": "The European pilchard, although not as abundant in our waters as in other parts of the Mediterranean, is nevertheless among the common small pelagic species of our country. It lives in large schools at depths down to 100 metres during the day, and rises closer to the surface at night. The pilchard reaches 25 centimetres in length, but individuals of the species caught in our waters usually do not exceed 15 centimetres. It feeds on plankton, filtering the water as it swims. The breeding season of the pilchard in our waters is not clearly defined, because it depends to a large extent on the sea temperature. The pilchard is caught with static nets and bottom trawls. Although it does not have great commercial value, its flesh is preferred by many, both for its distinctive taste and for its nutritional value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the pilchard in the Mediterranean at 11 centimetres.", "Sarpa salpa": "The cow bream, known as \"sarpa\" or \"salpa\", is among the common bottom-dwelling fish, although in recent years, with the rapid increase in spinefoot populations (Siganus spp.) and the decline of the seaweeds for which the two species compete, its population has declined significantly. It occurs mainly in areas with rich vegetation and rocky bottoms. It is found at depths between 1 and 70 metres, but you are most likely to meet it between 5 and 20 metres. The cow bream is a social fish and forms large schools with individuals of its own species of similar size. The length of this fish usually ranges from 15 to 25 centimetres, although it can exceed 40 centimetres. It is a fish that feeds almost exclusively on plant organisms, but when young it is carnivorous and shows a particular preference for crustaceans. During the summer, eating fish that have consumed seaweed of the genus Caulerpa may cause food poisoning and stomach upsets in humans. The species breeds in two seasons. The first starts in April and ends in May, while the second starts in early October and lasts until the end of November. The cow bream is a hermaphrodite and matures sexually when its length reaches about 16 centimetres, initially as a male. Then, at 3 years of age and after reaching a length of 25 centimetres, it changes sex and becomes a female (protandry). It is caught mainly with static nets, encircling nets, \"skarkes\", spearguns and rods. It does not have great commercial value, because it does not keep for long on the fishmongers' counters, and also because of its diet, since it has long, almost always full intestines, which makes it not very pleasant-smelling. Nevertheless, if eaten fresh, it is quite tasty.", "Trachurus sp.": "In our waters there are two species with the common name \"savridi\" (horse mackerel), Trachurus trachurus and Trachurus mediterraneus. These two species are very similar in both their shape and biology, and for this reason they are described here as one species. The horse mackerel is a common small pelagic species that lives in our waters, usually in large schools. In the summer months we usually meet it at depths no greater than 100 metres, while in winter it migrates to deeper water, down to 500 metres. The horse mackerel reaches 40 centimetres in length, but is usually smaller, up to 25 centimetres. It feeds on small fish, crustaceans and cephalopods. It breeds in spring. The horse mackerel is caught mainly with static nets and bottom trawls, as well as with fine surface trolling lines, handlines and \"tsapari\" (feather rigs). Its flesh is of average quality and does not have great commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the horse mackerel in the Mediterranean at 15 centimetres.", "Sciaena umbra": "The brown meagre, known as \"siakos\", \"sykios\" or \"oumbrella\", is a bottom-dwelling species that lives mainly on rocky bottoms at depths from 5 to 100 metres. It is a nocturnal species that usually hides in rock cavities during the day. Juveniles are often seen near Posidonia meadows. The meagre can reach 50 centimetres in length and 3 kilos in weight, but usually does not exceed 35 centimetres in length and 1 kilo in weight. It feeds mainly on small fish, invertebrates and molluscs. It breeds from March to August. During the breeding season, males produce a strong characteristic sound caused by contractions of their swim bladder. The meagre is fished with static nets, longlines, rods, \"skarkes\" and spearguns and has very good-quality flesh and high commercial value.", "Spondyliosoma cantharus": "The black seabream, known as \"skathari\" or \"moutsiosorkos\" in some parts of Cyprus, occurs in all areas of the island, although it is not among the very common species. It is a species found in habitats rich in vegetation, in sandy or even rocky coastal areas. We usually find it at depths between 5 and 150 metres, although we may also meet it down to 300 metres. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). The black seabream is quite an active fish that likes to form packs with its own kind, but also with \"sorkoi\", \"charatzides\" and saddled seabream. Its length usually ranges from 20 to 30 centimetres, although it can reach 60 centimetres and its weight can exceed 1 kilo. It feeds mainly on seaweed and crustaceans, but also on invertebrates. The breeding season of the black seabream starts in February and ends in May. It belongs to the protogynous hermaphrodite species. It matures sexually for the first time when its length reaches about 20 centimetres. At first sexual maturity, all individuals with very few exceptions are female and produce eggs. Then, as it grows, the seabream can change into a male and produce sperm. Unlike other related species, the black seabream lays few eggs. Specifically, it places them in a nest on the bottom, where the male patiently guards them until they hatch. As seen in the pictures, there is sexual dimorphism, that is, the male and the female differ in shape. The black seabream is caught mainly with longlines, static nets and handlines. It has tasty flesh and high commercial value.", "Synodus saurus": "The bluestriped lizardfish, known in some parts of Cyprus as \"skarmos\", \"papanikolis\" or \"nikolis\", is a very common fish in the waters of our island. It is a benthic species and lives in areas with vegetated rocky bottoms, but mainly occurs in areas with sandy-muddy substrates. We usually find it at depths between 0 and 50 metres, although it has also been recorded at much greater depths, beyond 200 metres. It is an opportunistic hunter and lies in wait camouflaged in the sand, waiting for its prey to approach. Its length usually ranges from 15 to 25 centimetres, although it can exceed 30 centimetres. It is a carnivorous predatory fish and feeds on small fish and invertebrates. The lizardfish does not hesitate to attack baits larger than itself. The breeding season of the species starts in February and ends in August. It is caught with static nets, handlines, bottom trolling lines, lures and rods from the shore. It has little commercial value and is sold at fishmongers, mixed with other species, at a low price.", "Sparisome cretense": "The parrotfish, known as \"skaros\", is a fish that occurs relatively often along the coasts of Cyprus. It is a benthic species and we find it in areas with meadows of Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica), on sandy and muddy substrates and also on rocky bottoms, not very far from the shore, at depths between 1 and 50 metres. The length of this fish can exceed 40 centimetres, but usually ranges between 10 and 25 centimetres. The parrotfish feeds on small invertebrates, algae and seaweed. The breeding season of the species begins in July and ends around the end of September. During the breeding season the species schools and approaches the coast. As seen in the pictures, the parrotfish shows sexual dimorphism, since the male and the female differ in shape. It is caught with static nets, encircling nets, \"skarkes\", rods and spearguns. The flesh of the parrotfish is excellent and is preferred by gourmets, who usually eat the parrotfish with its entrails. It has high commercial value, in proportion to the size of the fish.", "Scorpaena scrofa": "In Cyprus there are five different fish species that are given the name \"skorpios\" (scorpionfish). They look very much alike, so there is some ambiguity about telling them apart. In general they have a reddish colouring and large fins with venomous spines. Besides the identical appearance, these species also have similar biological behaviour. For these reasons, these fish are known as scorpionfish and are not separated at the fishmongers. Scorpaena scrofa is the one found most often in Cypriot waters, which is why it was chosen to be described in the pages of this book. The scorpionfish is a very common fish in the waters of Cyprus. It is a purely benthic species and occurs in rocky areas rich in holes, as well as in areas with sandy-muddy substrates. This scorpionfish is the largest of all the species in the scorpionfish family. It can reach 50 centimetres in length and its weight can exceed 2 kilos. Its length usually ranges between 15 and 30 centimetres. It is a sluggish fish that is almost always found resting on the bottom, since it has no swim bladder. It occurs at depths from 2 to 200 metres. Smaller individuals find shelter in shallow water, between 2 and 30 metres, while larger individuals prefer deeper water. It is an opportunistic, solitary hunter that lies in wait, camouflaged, for its prey to approach. It feeds on molluscs, crustaceans and fish, even smaller scorpionfish of the same species. The breeding season of the species takes place in spring. The first spines of the first dorsal fin are venomous. There are also spines on the gill covers, which are just as dangerous and harmful to humans as those on the dorsal fin. Despite their differences, all scorpionfish species of this family cause injuries and must be handled with care. The scorpionfish is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, longlines, handlines and spearguns. It is not considered a discard, but it is often discarded because of its size. Its commercial value is in proportion to its size.", "Helicolenus dactylopterus": "As already mentioned, five species of scorpionfish, as the fish of this family are called on our island, have so far been identified in Cyprus. They look very much alike, so there is some ambiguity about telling these species apart. In general they have a reddish colouring and large fins with venomous spines. For these reasons, these fish are known as scorpionfish and are not separated at the fishmongers. Nevertheless, Helicolenus dactylopterus stands out from the others and is perhaps the only one that is sometimes separated at some fishmongers. The blackbelly rosefish H. dactylopterus, known in Greece as \"lipsos\", is a common fish in the waters of Cyprus. It is a purely benthic species and occurs in rocky areas rich in holes, but also on sandy-muddy substrates. Its length can reach 40 centimetres and its weight can exceed 2 kilos. Its length usually ranges between 15 and 25 centimetres. It is a sluggish fish that is almost always found resting on the bottom. The first spines of the first dorsal fin are venomous. It occurs at depths from 2 to 500 metres, with smaller individuals of the species finding shelter in water between 50 and 100 metres. It is an opportunistic, solitary hunter that lies in wait, camouflaged, for its prey to approach. It feeds on molluscs, crustaceans, echinoderms and fish. The breeding season of the species takes place between February and March. The rosefish is caught with longlines, handlines and sometimes static nets. Its commercial value is in proportion to its size.", "Scomber scombrus": "The Atlantic mackerel is a fairly common small pelagic species that approaches the coasts of our country and forms large schools near the surface in the summer months. In the winter months it moves to deeper water, down to 250 metres. The mackerel can reach 50 centimetres in length, although it rarely exceeds 30 centimetres. It feeds mainly on zooplankton and small fish in the water column. It breeds in the summer months. The mackerel is caught in large quantities in summer by professional fishermen, mainly with static nets and bottom trawls. It is also fished by amateur fishermen with fine trolling lines, handlines and \"tsapari\" (feather rigs). Its flesh is of quite good quality, although because of its abundance it does not have great commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the mackerel in the Mediterranean at 18 centimetres.", "Schyliorhinus canicula": "The lesser spotted dogfish, known as \"skylopsaraki\", is one of the species that occur often in the waters of Cyprus. It is a bottom-dwelling fish and lives on sandy-muddy substrates at depths from 5 to 400 metres, but mainly occurs at depths between 50 and 150 metres. The length of individuals of this species usually ranges from 20 to 50 centimetres, although it can reach 1 metre. The dogfish is a nocturnal species that rests on the sand during the day and becomes active at night to find food, which consists mainly of molluscs, crustaceans and bottom-dwelling fish. The species breeds around the end of summer in deep water. Then, between November and July, the females approach the coast to lay their eggs (transparent capsules), usually on seaweed. The eggs hatch in about 9 months and at hatching the young dogfish are about 10 centimetres long. It is caught with static nets, longlines and bottom trawls. It has no commercial value and is discarded.", "Muraena helena": "The Mediterranean moray, known as \"smerna\" or \"smyrna\", is a fairly common species that occurs in the coastal waters of Cyprus at depths from 0 to 100 metres. Its length usually ranges from 50 to 100 centimetres, although it can exceed 120. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds on fish, molluscs and crustaceans. It is one of the few fish in the coastal area of Cyprus that can injure humans, since it has jaws armed with very thin, sharp teeth that slant inwards. When these close, it is not easy to open them. Once caught, the moray reacts violently, biting any object near it. Its bite is dangerous and painful. Nevertheless, the moray is anything but aggressive, since most of the day it is hidden in rock cavities and does not attack unless it feels threatened. The moray, as mentioned above, lives near rocky areas, where it finds shelter in holes during the day. At night it comes out to hunt bottom-dwelling fish and molluscs. It is a gonochoristic species and its breeding season takes place between February and July, depending on the sea temperature. It is caught with static nets, longlines, handlines, rods and spearguns. Despite the dangers involved in fishing for moray, its flesh is very tasty, superior to that of the conger. It is generally very tasty if it receives the proper preparation (\"galema\"). Nevertheless, because of its appearance and the special preparation required for cooking it, this species has very little commercial value and is very often discarded by fishermen and does not end up at the fishmongers.", "Diplodus sargus": "The white seabream, known as \"sorkos\", is a fairly common bottom-dwelling species that occurs mainly in areas with a rocky substrate. It likes to hide in dark holes with individuals of its own species, but also other species such as saddled seabream, brown meagre, \"stires\", groupers and others. Unlike most bottom-dwelling species, the white seabream remains, even today, one of the species available in adequate supply on our island, perhaps because of its great adaptability to every type of bottom, the migratory behaviour it shows in the winter months, and its quite high intelligence. Although not often, the white seabream also occurs in sandy areas. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). It is found at depths not exceeding 50 metres and its length usually ranges from 15 to 30 centimetres, although it can reach 45 centimetres and its weight can exceed 2 kilos. It feeds on worms, crustaceans, molluscs, shellfish and occasionally algae. It is quite an active and mobile fish, especially in the evening and morning hours, when it leaves its hole more often to hunt. The breeding season of the species starts in December and ends in February. The white seabream is a hermaphrodite and matures sexually at 2 to 3 years of age, initially as a male, producing only sperm. Then, during the process of changing sex, it briefly produces both eggs and sperm. If it lives long enough, it changes completely into a female (protandry). It is caught mainly with static nets, fine longlines, handlines, rods, \"skarkes\" and spearguns. It has tasty flesh and quite high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the white seabream in the Mediterranean at 23 centimetres.", "Diplodus cervinus": "The zebra seabream, known as \"sorkos faraos\" or simply \"faraos\", is a bottom-dwelling fish species that is not particularly common in the waters of Cyprus. It occurs mainly in areas with rocky substrates, where it likes to hide in dark holes. We may also meet it on sandy bottoms, particularly at great depth. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). It is found at depths between 10 and 60 metres, although there are indications that it reaches 300 metres. It likes to form small packs with individuals of its own species, regardless of size. Its length usually ranges from 30 to 40 centimetres, although it can reach 60 centimetres and its weight can exceed 5 kilos. It is an omnivorous fish and feeds mainly on seaweed, algae and small invertebrates. The breeding season of the species starts in December and ends around the end of March. It is caught mainly with longlines, handlines, spearguns and sometimes static nets. It has quite high commercial value.", "Caranx crysos": "The blue runner, known as \"souros\", is a coastal species with pelagic behaviour that occurs in schools at depths down to 100 metres, but usually down to 30 metres. It can reach 70 centimetres in length and 5 kilos in weight, although it usually does not exceed 35 centimetres. The runner feeds on small fish, shrimps, crabs and other invertebrates. It breeds from January to August. The runner is caught mainly with static nets and has very good-quality flesh. In our waters there is another species that looks like the blue runner, Alepes djedaba. This species is of Indo-Pacific origin and a Lessepsian migrant to the Mediterranean. A. djedaba is smaller than the blue runner, since it usually does not exceed 25 centimetres in length. This species is also found in schools in coastal waters.", "Diplodus annularis": "The annular seabream, known as \"sparos\", is one of the most common species living along the coasts of our island. It is a bottom-dwelling species and occurs mainly near meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica), on sandy and muddy substrates and rarely in rocky areas, at depths from 0 to 90 metres. Its length ranges from 6 to 15 centimetres. It feeds on worms, echinoderms, crustaceans, molluscs and hydrozoans. The breeding season of the species starts in February and ends in April. Without being a rule, the annular seabream has the capacity for hermaphroditism. Specifically, most individuals of the species first reach sexual maturity as males and over time change into females (protandry). The annular seabream matures sexually within 1 year and can reproduce. It is caught mainly with static nets, fine longlines, \"skarkes\", handlines and rods. It has little commercial value, mainly because of its small size. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the annular seabream in the Mediterranean at 12 centimetres.", "Epinephelus costae": "The goldblotch grouper, known as \"stira\", is a bottom-dwelling species that usually occurs on rocky or mixed bottoms with a steep slope next to deep water, usually at depths from 20 to 100 metres. It can exceed 1 metre in length and 10 kilos in weight, although it usually does not exceed 50 centimetres in length and 3 kilos in weight. The goldblotch grouper, like most members of its family, usually uses cavities in rock formations for protection from its predators. It usually moves in small groups and feeds on fish, cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid) and crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, etc.). It is a protogynous hermaphrodite. It matures sexually as a female when its length reaches about 40 centimetres, and changes into a male when it reaches about 60 centimetres. It breeds in summer. The goldblotch grouper has good-quality flesh and is caught mainly with heavy longlines, bottom trolling lines, lures, handlines and spearguns. More rarely it is also caught in fishermen's static nets. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the goldblotch grouper in the Mediterranean at 45 centimetres.", "Mullus barbatus": "The red mullet, known as \"strilia\" or \"koutsomoura\", is a small bottom-dwelling species that occurs mainly on muddy or sandy bottoms and at depths down to 350 metres. Many confuse it with the striped red mullet because of their great similarity. The main morphological feature that separates the two species is the much more curved snout of the red mullet compared with that of the striped red mullet. It is usually about 15 centimetres long, and can reach 30 centimetres. It feeds on small benthic invertebrates such as crustaceans, worms and molluscs, which, like the striped red mullet, it locates in the substrate with its barbels. The red mullet breeds from April to August. It is caught with bottom trawls and static nets and, like the striped red mullet, is considered a species of excellent quality. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the red mullet in the Mediterranean at 11 centimetres.", "Etrumeus teres": "The round herring, known as \"strogyloregga\", is a small pelagic species that forms schools at depths down to 150 metres. It is a Lessepsian migrant, that is, it entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea, and its presence is becoming more and more frequent in our waters. The round herring is usually about 25 centimetres long and can exceed 30 centimetres. It feeds on planktonic microorganisms. The breeding season of the round herring in our waters is not yet known. This species is caught with static nets and bottom trawls and its flesh is of average quality.", "Dentex dentex": "The common dentex, known as \"synagrida\", belongs to the category of common bottom-dwelling fish of Cyprus and occurs in all areas of Cyprus. It usually lives near rocky areas with holes and rich vegetation, near shoals and shipwrecks, and we may also meet it in areas with a sandy substrate. It is high in the food chain and is among the top predators. It is usually found at depths between 15 and 50 metres, but we may also meet it in completely shallow water or at depths reaching 200 metres. It forms small schools with individuals of its own species, but often acts as a solitary hunter, especially as it grows older. Its length usually ranges from 20 to 50 centimetres, although it can exceed 1 metre and weigh more than 12 kilos. As mentioned above, the dentex is a hunter and feeds mainly on fish and cephalopods. The breeding season of the species takes place in spring, from March to May. The dentex matures sexually at 2 to 3 years and is a gonochoristic species, although hermaphroditism has been observed in isolated cases. It is caught mainly with static nets, longlines, bottom trawls, bottom trolling lines, lures, handlines and spearguns and has very high commercial value. Unfortunately, dentex populations still come under enormous pressure from illegal fishing with scuba gear and spearguns, particularly at night, when the fish is very vulnerable. Fortunately, however, today this practice is not accepted as it was in the old days, but is considered by most to be a cowardly and reprehensible act.", "Epinephelus aeneus": "The white grouper, known as \"sfyrida\", is a bottom-dwelling species that occurs on mixed muddy bottoms at depths from about 10 to 100 metres. In Cyprus it is absent from some areas, while it is often found in others. Many fishermen confuse it with the dogtooth grouper (Epinephelus caninus), which it resembles in some respects. The white grouper has the habit of building its own den, digging burrows in the soft substrate, under slabs or clumps of Posidonia. It reaches 115 centimetres in length and 25 kilos in weight, although its usual length is about 50 centimetres and its weight about 3 kilos. It feeds mainly on fish, cephalopods (octopus, cuttlefish, squid) and crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, etc.). Like most of its related species, the white grouper is a protogynous hermaphrodite, that is, it starts its sexual life as a female and changes into a male near the age of 10 years, when it is about 80 centimetres long and weighs 8 kilos. It breeds in spring. The white grouper is caught mainly with heavy longlines, handlines, bottom trolling lines, lures, spearguns and, with more difficulty, static nets and bottom trawls. Its flesh is considered of excellent quality, which is why its commercial value is high. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the white grouper in the Mediterranean at 45 centimetres.", "Sphyraena sphyraena": "The European barracuda, known as \"sfyrna\" or \"loutsos\", is a coastal species that usually moves in large schools from the surface down to 50 metres. Its presence is stronger near capes, shoals, shipwrecks, even inside harbours. The barracuda can exceed 1 metre in length, but usually does not exceed 70 centimetres in length and 2 kilos in weight. It is an excellent hunter and feeds mainly on fish and, less often, on cephalopods and crustaceans. It breeds in the summer months. The barracuda is fished with static nets, longlines, surface trolling lines and spearguns. Its flesh is considered of very good quality. In recent years two more barracuda species have appeared in our waters, Sphyraena chrysotaenia and Sphyraena obtusata, which are Lessepsian migrants, that is, they entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea. These barracudas are generally smaller than S. sphyraena.", "Thunnus alalunga": "The albacore, known as \"tonaki\" or \"long-finned tuna\", is a relatively small tuna species that shows strongly migratory behaviour. It is a pelagic species and moves in schools from the surface down to 600 metres. Huge schools of albacore flood the surface waters of our country in the summer months during the breeding season. At this time, the presence of the schools is generally stronger in areas where there are warmer water masses. The albacore usually does not exceed 1 metre in length and 15 kilos in weight, although larger individuals have occasionally been caught in our waters. The albacore is a fast hunter that often hunts in groups and feeds mainly on small pelagic fish species, squid and some crustacean species. The breeding season for the albacore coincides with its fishing season in our waters, that is, from June to August. The albacore is caught en masse with surface longlines by professional fishermen and with surface trolling lines using artificial lures by amateurs. In our country there are a large number of amateur fishermen who are passionate about fishing for this species, mainly because of its abundance and the strong fight it gives when hooked. Although the albacore is highly valued for its meat in many foreign countries, in Cyprus it does not have great commercial value. For this reason, the larger quantities caught by our professional fishermen are intended for export. Under national legislation, amateur fishermen are allowed to catch up to three individuals of the species per fishing trip.", "Thunnus thynnus": "The Atlantic bluefin tuna, known as \"touna\", \"koinos tonos\" or \"erythros tonos\" (red tuna, because of the red colour of its flesh), is one of the largest and strongest fish living in the waters of our country. It is a pelagic species that usually moves in schools from the surface of the water down to a depth of more than 500 metres. It is a strongly migratory species that covers huge distances to travel from its breeding areas to its hunting grounds. The bluefin is a fast-growing fish, since it can reach 3 metres in length and 700 kilos in weight at the age of just 15 years. Usually, however, tunas caught in our waters do not exceed 300 kilos in weight. The bluefin is a skilful hunter that often hunts in groups and feeds mainly on fish and cephalopods. The bluefin breeds in our waters in June, July and August. It is caught with special surface longlines, trolling lines and heavy handlines. It is also caught with encircling nets (purse seines), with the aim of later fattening it (improving the quality of the meat, mainly by increasing its fat content) in open-sea cages and exporting it abroad. The flesh of the bluefin is of very good quality and in some countries, mainly Japan, this fish has enormous commercial value. By way of illustration, in a Japanese market in 1996, a 238-kilo fish was sold for 54,000 US dollars. The enormous fishing pressure exerted on this species, mainly because of its great value in Japan, has led to a huge decline of its populations across its whole range. Unfortunately, because of the strongly migratory nature of this species, the exploited stocks are shared among many countries, and so taking measures to recover the stocks becomes even more difficult.", "Upeneus moluccensis": "The goldband goatfish, known as \"tourkobarbouno\", \"tourkou\" or \"lochias\", is a Lessepsian species, that is, it migrated into the Mediterranean from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal. It is a small bottom-dwelling species that occurs mainly on muddy bottoms and at depths down to 120 metres. It is easily distinguished from the striped red mullet and the red mullet by the characteristic yellow lines along the sides of its body. It is usually about 15 centimetres long, and can reach 25 centimetres. It forms large schools and feeds on benthic microorganisms, which it locates in the substrate with its barbels. Like the striped red mullet and the red mullet, the goldband goatfish is caught with bottom trawls and static nets and is considered a species of very good quality. It is worth noting that another Lessepsian species of the same genus as U. moluccensis lives in our waters, Upeneus pori. This species resembles U. moluccensis in shape but does not have the characteristic yellow line on the side of its body.", "Sparus aurata": "Although today the gilthead seabream, known as \"tsipoura\", is perhaps the best-known fish to the average Cypriot, the natural populations in Cypriot waters are limited. The gilthead seabream owes its fame to aquaculture, where this species, at least at national level, has become dominant. Most of the gilthead seabream caught from time to time along our island's coasts have most probably escaped from their fattening cages. Nevertheless, whether from accidental escapes into the sea or from the small population of wild individuals of the species, this species is noteworthy. The gilthead seabream is a bottom-dwelling fish that occurs mainly in areas with rocky and muddy substrates, but also in areas with rich vegetation. It is a species that lives alone or forms schools of its own kind. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). It is usually found at depths not exceeding 30 metres, although mainly adult individuals occur at depths down to 150 metres. The length of the gilthead seabream usually ranges from 25 to 35 centimetres, although it can exceed 50 centimetres. Its weight can exceed 6 kilos. It is an omnivorous fish and feeds on worms, crustaceans, molluscs, shellfish, small fish and occasionally algae and seaweed. The breeding season of the species starts in October and ends in December. The gilthead seabream is a hermaphrodite and matures sexually at 1 to 2 years of age, initially as a male, producing sperm. Then, during the process of changing sex, it briefly produces both eggs and sperm and, if it lives long enough, at 2 to 3 years of age it changes completely into a female (protandry). It is caught with static nets, fine longlines, handlines, rods and spearguns. It has relatively low commercial value, which rarely changes because of its continuous and steady production by Cypriot fish farms. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the gilthead seabream in the Mediterranean at 20 centimetres.", "Ruvettus pretiosus": "The oilfish, known as \"tsiritzi\", \"ladopsaro\", \"pilakoutas\" or \"rouvettos\", is a large benthopelagic species that lives at depths from 100 to 800 metres. It reaches 2 metres in length and 60 kilos in weight, but usually individuals of the species fished in our waters are about 1 metre long and weigh less than 15 kilos. It moves alone or in pairs, usually near the bottom, and often rises to mid-water to feed. The oilfish feeds mainly on fish, cephalopods and crustaceans. Unfortunately, there is no data available on the breeding season of this species in our waters. The oilfish is fished with heavy longlines, either surface longlines or bottom longlines. Its flesh, although tasty, is very oily and, if eaten in large quantities, can cause stomach upsets and diarrhoea because of a toxin it contains.", "Pagrus pagrus": "The common seabream, known as \"fagri\" (red porgy), is among the most common species that inhabit the habitats of Cypriot seas. It is a benthic species and lives mainly in areas with clear water and rocky bottoms rich in holes that provide cover and safety, and it also occurs in areas with sandy-muddy substrates. Juveniles find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). We usually find it at depths between 10 and 80 metres, although it can reach 250 metres. Juveniles live mainly in shallow water, while adults prefer greater depths, although in the winter months they move offshore, mainly because of a lack of food. The seabream likes to form small packs of its own kind. Its length usually ranges from 20 to 45 centimetres, although it can reach 80 centimetres and its weight can exceed 7 kilos. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds on small fish, molluscs and crustaceans. The breeding season of the species begins in April and ends around the end of June. The seabream is a protogynous hermaphrodite species and matures sexually for the first time in its third year of age. It is caught with static nets, longlines, handlines, bottom trolling lines and lures. It has high commercial value, which increases significantly as its size increases. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the red porgy in the Mediterranean at 18 centimetres.", "Dentex gibbosus": "The pink dentex, known as \"koronato fagri\", \"tsaousis\" or simply \"koronato\", is a bottom-dwelling species that occurs in rocky areas with holes, near shoals or even in areas with a sandy substrate. In juveniles of the species the third and fourth rays of the dorsal fin are much longer than the rest, while adult females carry a characteristic hump (bump) a little above the level of the eye, which is how it got its common name. The pink dentex is high in the food chain and is among the top predators. It occurs at depths between 15 and 250 metres, with juveniles preferring shallow water and adults tending towards deeper water. Quite often it forms schools of its own kind, but it also acts as a solitary hunter. The length of the species usually ranges from 35 to 60 centimetres, but it can reach 1 metre and its weight can exceed 25 kilos. The pink dentex is carnivorous and feeds on crustaceans, fish and cephalopods. The species breeds from early March to early June. It is among the protandrous hermaphrodite species. It is caught mainly with longlines, handlines, bottom trolling lines, lures and, more rarely, static nets. It has very high commercial value.", "Pempheris vanicolensis": "The Vanikoro sweeper, known as \"fatsoui\", is a small bottom-dwelling fish that prefers hard substrates at depths down to 30 metres. It is often seen living in small groups at the entrance to or inside rock cavities. Its length can reach 18 centimetres, but usually does not exceed 12 centimetres. The sweeper is a nocturnal species, since at night it leaves the cavities where it lives in search of food. It feeds on planktonic microorganisms, mainly invertebrates. The sweeper breeds in our waters from April to September. It has no commercial value, but it is often caught as bycatch in fishermen's static nets.", "Pagellus acarne": "The axillary bream, known as \"fatsoukli\", is a common fish in the waters of our island. It is a benthic species and occurs on a variety of substrates, but mainly in areas with rich vegetation and sandy bottoms. It is found down to 500 metres. Adults live mainly near the bottom at depths from 60 to 200 metres, while juveniles are usually found in shallower water. The axillary bream likes to form large schools of its own kind, especially in late summer and in the autumn months, when it also breeds. Its length usually ranges from 10 to 18 centimetres, although it can reach 30 centimetres. It is an omnivorous fish and feeds mainly on crustaceans, worms, molluscs and small fish, but sometimes it also consumes seaweed. The breeding season of the species begins in August and ends in November. The axillary bream is a protandrous hermaphrodite fish, with a length at first sexual maturity of about 10 centimetres. It changes into a female, usually in its second or third year of life, when its length ranges from 13 to 18 centimetres. It is easily caught with static nets and handlines, and it is also a target species for bottom trawls because of its abundance and its social nature. Because it is usually small and is at times caught en masse, it has little commercial value. Large individuals of the species have higher commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the axillary bream in the Mediterranean at 17 centimetres.", "Sardinella aurita": "The round sardinella, known as \"frissa\", is a small pelagic species that resembles the sardine and forms schools at depths down to 350 metres, and is often seen rising higher during the night. It is generally larger than the sardine, since it can reach 30 centimetres in length, although it usually does not exceed 20 centimetres. Like the sardine, it feeds on planktonic microorganisms, filtering the water as it swims. It breeds in July, August and September. The sardinella is caught with static nets and bottom trawls, and its flesh is not considered particularly good quality.", "Serranus cabrilla": "The comber, known as \"channos\", is a small bottom-dwelling species that occurs in abundance on all types of bottom and at depths down to 500 metres. It is perhaps the best-known fish to our country's amateur fishermen, because of its abundance and the ease with which it is caught on a hook. It can exceed 30 centimetres in length, although it usually ranges from 10 to 20 centimetres. It feeds on small fish, crustaceans, molluscs and worms. The comber is a simultaneous hermaphrodite species and breeding takes place from April to July. It is caught with bottom trawls, static nets, \"skarkes\" and fine handlines, and its flesh is not considered particularly good quality. Another species that looks like the comber, but is much smaller, is the brown comber (Serranus hepatus), known as \"channoui\". The brown comber lives on all types of bottom, at depths from 5 to 100 metres, and rarely exceeds 10 centimetres in length. It feeds on smaller fish, crustaceans and molluscs. It breeds from March to August. The brown comber has no commercial value, mainly because of its small size, but it is caught as bycatch by bottom trawls.", "Diplodus vulgaris": "The two-banded seabream, known as \"charatzida\" or \"kakarelos\", is a fairly common bottom-dwelling fish. It occurs mainly in areas with rocky or sandy substrates and usually at depths not exceeding 50 metres, although it can sometimes exceed 150. Juveniles often find shelter in meadows of the marine flowering plant Posidonia (Posidonia oceanica). Its length usually ranges between 17 and 25 centimetres, although it can reach 45 centimetres. It is a fish that forms schools with a small number of individuals and feeds on worms, crustaceans, plants and molluscs. The breeding season of the species starts in December and ends in January. The two-banded seabream has the capacity for hermaphroditism. Specifically, it first matures as a male when its length reaches 17 centimetres. Then the larger individuals of the species change into females (protandry), depending on the numerical balance between males and females in the school or area. It is caught mainly with longlines, static nets, \"skarkes\", handlines, rods and spearguns. It has quite high commercial value. European regulations set the minimum catch size (total length) for the two-banded seabream in the Mediterranean at 18 centimetres.", "Symphodus tinca": "In Cyprus there are several fish species with the common name \"cheilou\" (wrasse). They resemble each other, and for some there is some ambiguity about how to tell them apart. Because of the relatively large number of these species that we meet in Cyprus, and because of the low commercial value of most of them, they are not separated and so all carry the general name mentioned above. Also, several of them have identical biological behaviour and habits. Symphodus tinca, the peacock wrasse, is perhaps the one found most often in Cypriot waters. It is a purely benthic species and lives in rocky areas, as well as in areas with vegetation. It can reach 40 centimetres, but its length usually ranges between 10 and 25 centimetres. It occurs at depths from 1 to 50 metres. It feeds on molluscs, crustaceans and sea urchins. The species breeds between March and June. During this period the males acquire more intense colour and build nests, usually in areas with seaweed. There the females, usually 1 or 2, lay their eggs and leave, leaving the guarding of the eggs to the male. This species has the ability to change its sex under certain conditions. It is caught with static nets, handlines, rods, \"skarkes\" and spearguns. Unlike other species of the same family, it is not considered a discard, but it has quite low commercial value. Another common wrasse in the coastal waters of Cyprus is Symphodus mediterraneus. We find it in rocky areas, as well as in areas with vegetation and algae. Its length usually ranges between 10 and 12 centimetres, although it can reach 18 centimetres. It occurs at depths from 1 to 50 metres. It feeds on bivalve molluscs, gastropods, crustaceans, worms and sea urchins. It moves in pairs or forms small schools of its own kind and sometimes of individuals of the same family. The species breeds in the summer months. During this period the males acquire more intense colour and build nests, usually in areas with seaweed. There the females, usually 1 or 2, lay their eggs and leave, leaving the guarding of the eggs to the male.", "Parexocoetus mento": "The African sailfin flyingfish, known as \"chelidonopsaro\", is a Lessepsian species, that is, it entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal. This species is known for its ability to fly above the surface of the water for quite long distances. This ability helps it escape from would-be predators. We find it at depths down to 20 metres, from coastal waters to the open sea. The flyingfish is usually about 10 centimetres long, and can reach 15 centimetres. Although there is no data on the diet of this species, it most likely feeds on zooplankton and small fish. The flyingfish breeds in the summer months. It is caught occasionally in fishermen's static nets and its flesh is of average quality.", "Balistes capriscus": "The grey triggerfish, known as \"choiros\" or \"choiropsaro\" (pig fish), is a benthic species that lives mainly on rocky substrates and in areas with rich vegetation. It also occurs quite often near shipwrecks or under large floating objects. It lives at depths from 1 to 100 metres. It is a solitary fish that does not form schools but lives alone or with its mate. The length of the triggerfish usually ranges from 15 to 35 centimetres. It has the ability to grow quite large. There are records in the Mediterranean of individuals of the species reaching 5 kilos in weight and 50 centimetres in length. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds on benthic invertebrates such as molluscs and crustaceans. The species breeds in the summer months. During breeding, the female lays her eggs in a small cavity in the muddy substrate, where the male then guards them until they hatch. It is caught with static nets, longlines, handlines and sometimes trolling lines. The triggerfish has very tough skin, and a process of removing it is required before eating, which may also be the reason for its low commercial value. Besides B. capriscus, another fish species in our waters has the common name choiros. This is Stephanolepis diaspros, the reticulated leatherjacket, also called \"monakanthos\", which is a Lessepsian migrant, that is, it entered the Mediterranean from the Red Sea through the Suez Canal. S. diaspros is a benthic species that lives mainly on rocky substrates and in areas with rich vegetation. It occurs mainly in the coastal zone at depths from 5 to 50 metres. It likes to form small schools of its own kind, but we may also meet it alone. Its length usually ranges from 7 to 15 centimetres. It is a carnivorous fish and feeds mainly on small invertebrates, which it tears from the rocks with its powerful incisors. Unfortunately, there is no data on the breeding of this species. It is caught with static nets. It has very low commercial value and, because of its small size and tough skin, in most cases it is discarded."};
